---
import LabAssessment from "../../components/LabAssessment.astro";
import { MAL_ENTENDIDOS_DA_REDE, NOME_DA_FICHA } from "@ovh/network-domain";
import { RedeModos } from "../../components/RedeModos.tsx";
import { Predicao } from "../../components/Predicao.tsx";
import SiteFooter from "../../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import SiteNav from "../../components/SiteNav.astro";
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { url } from "../../lib/urls";
import "../../styles/docs.css";
import "../../components/ModosDoLab.css";
import "../../components/CpuVitrine.css";
import "../../components/ContextoLab.css";
import "../../components/ImplantacaoLab.css";
import "../../components/RedeLab.css";
import "@ovh/motion/src/react/motion.css";
import "@ovh/stage3d/src/iso.css";
import "../../components/Predicao.css";
---

<Base
  domain="networks"
  title="One Wi-Fi, three rooms — OpenLabs"
  description="A community telecentre network, packet by packet: DHCP, DNS, HTTP, inter-VLAN routing over an 802.1Q trunk, NAT to the internet, an ACL that keeps the public Wi-Fi out of the staff network, and SSH — each step next to the same step in Cisco Packet Tracer."
>
  <SiteNav current="labs" />

  <main id="content">
    <section class="lab-head editorial">
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="lab-head__eyebrow mono">
          <a href={url("handbooks/networks")}>Networks Visual Handbook</a> · lab
        </p>
        <h1>What happens when a learner joins the telecentre Wi-Fi</h1>
        <p>
          A free community telecentre lends its Wi-Fi to anyone who wants to learn.
          A laptop arrives with no address, and a minute later it is reading
          <code>{NOME_DA_FICHA}</code> and browsing the internet — while the staff
          computers on the same switch stay out of its reach.
        </p>
        <p>
          Six protocols made that happen, and each one is a chapter: DHCP lends the
          address, DNS turns the name into a number, HTTP brings the page, NAT lets
          a private address reach the internet, an ACL decides what the Wi-Fi may
          touch, and SSH keeps the router for the staff. Every chapter shows the
          same step in Cisco Packet Tracer: the commands and the output to expect.
        </p>
      </div>
      <aside class="lab-head__note mono">
        <p>In 3D, height is where a device decides:</p>
        <p>hosts on the floor, the switch (layer 2) above, the router (layer 3) higher, the ISP on top.</p>
      </aside>
    </section>

    <section class="lab-palco">
      <RedeModos client:only="react" />
    </section>

    <section class="lab-prosa editorial">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Break it</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <b>Turn the ACL off and watch the ACL chapter.</b> The ping from the
            Wi-Fi now reaches the staff PC: VLANs separate broadcast domains, but
            the router joins them unless something says no.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Turn NAT off and watch the NAT chapter.</b> The request reaches the
            website, but the reply is addressed to <code>192.168.x.x</code>, and the
            ISP has no route to a private address.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>In the simulation, ping the learner's laptop from the staff PC.</b>
            The request arrives; the reply is what the ACL drops. Isolation works
            in both directions.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Try SSH to R1 from the Wi-Fi.</b> The ACL lets it through — the vty
            lines refuse it. Two different guards, two different messages.
          </li>
        </ol>

        <h2>Predict before you look</h2>
        <p>Answer first, then try it in the simulation.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="lab-predicao">
        <Predicao
          client:visible
          pergunta="The learner's laptop pings the staff PC. The ACL on the Wi-Fi subinterface starts with “deny ip <Wi-Fi> <staff network>”. Where does the ping stop, and what does the laptop see?"
          opcoes={[
            "At R1, on the way in; the laptop sees “Destination host unreachable” from its gateway",
            "At the switch, because the two ports are on different VLANs; the laptop sees “Request timed out”",
            "At the staff PC, which ignores the ping; the laptop sees “Request timed out”",
          ]}
          correta={0}
          revelacao="The switch never decides between VLANs: it sends the frame up the trunk, tagged with VLAN 20, to the router. R1 checks the inbound ACL on G0/0.20, the first line matches, and the router drops the packet and answers with an ICMP unreachable — which the laptop prints as coming from its gateway."
        />
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="lab-prosa editorial">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>What people get wrong</h2>
        <p>Each row is a belief the simulation is built to take apart. The right column is the documentation, with the link.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="lab-mal-entendidos">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">What people believe</th>
              <th scope="col">What the docs say</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              MAL_ENTENDIDOS_DA_REDE.map((m) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>{m.crenca}</td>
                  <td>
                    {m.spec}{" "}
                    <a href={m.fonte} rel="noreferrer noopener" target="_blank">
                      source
                    </a>
                  </td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="lab-prosa editorial">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Build it for real in Packet Tracer</h2>
        <p>
          The <b>Simulation</b> tab has the full addressing plan and the
          configuration of every device, generated from the same model that draws
          the film. Paste them into R1, SW1 and the ISP router; set up the server,
          the access point and the hosts with the values listed; then run the
          tests from each chapter and compare your screenshots with the expected
          output. The step-by-step guide lives in the repository, in
          <code>labs/telecentro-network/README.md</code>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <LabAssessment href="labs/telecentro-network" />
  </main>

  <SiteFooter />
</Base>

<style>
  .lab-head {
    padding-block: var(--space-4) var(--space-2);
  }

  .lab-head__eyebrow {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-1);
    color: var(--accent);
    font-size: var(--size-step--1);
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  h1 {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-2);
    font-size: var(--size-step-2);
    max-width: 24ch;
  }

  .lab-head__note {
    align-self: end;
    color: var(--ink-muted);
    font-size: var(--size-step--1);
  }

  .lab-head__note p {
    margin: 0 0 0.35rem;
  }

  .lab-palco {
    margin-block: var(--space-2) var(--space-4);
  }

  .lab-prosa {
    padding-block-end: var(--space-4);
  }

  .lab-predicao {
    grid-column: 1 / -1;
    margin-block-start: var(--space-2);
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .lab-mal-entendidos {
    grid-column: 1 / -1;
    margin-block-start: var(--space-2);
    overflow-x: auto;
  }

  .lab-mal-entendidos table {
    border-collapse: collapse;
    width: 100%;
    font-size: var(--size-step--1);
  }

  .lab-mal-entendidos th,
  .lab-mal-entendidos td {
    border-block-end: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding: var(--space-1) var(--space-2);
    text-align: start;
    vertical-align: top;
  }
</style>
